BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-saddles", "after": "sec-introduction-p2", "title": "The gravitational path integral and its saddles", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In AdS/CFT the CFT partition function on a Euclidean manifold equals a path integral of gravity over all metrics whose conformal boundary is that manifold. When the bulk is weakly curved and Newton's constant is small, the path integral is dominated by its classical solutions, the saddles, each contributing $e^{-I/16\pi G_N}$ with $I$ its renormalized action. For a large $N$ CFT, $1/G_N$ grows like a power of $N$, so the saddle of least action wins by an exponentially large factor. Saddles can differ in topology, since only the boundary is fixed: a factor of the boundary can be filled in by a ball in the interior or kept as a sphere. When two saddles exchange the role of least action as a boundary parameter varies, the free energy has a kink, a phase transition. At finite $N$ both saddles contribute, and the kink is smoothed. The paper follows these saddles on a boundary of two spheres.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-symmetry", "after": "sec-setup-p1", "title": "Symmetry and ordinary differential equations", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A metric invariant under the rotations $SO(d_1+1)\times SO(d_2+1)$ of both spheres has, at each point, an orbit that is a copy of $S^{d_1}\times S^{d_2}$, with only the two radii free to vary. The space is then foliated by these orbits along a single transverse direction, so the metric is fixed by functions of one variable: the radii $f$ and $h$, plus a radial component that a choice of coordinate sets to one. Einstein's equations, partial differential equations in general, become ordinary differential equations in that variable, with one constraint from the radial equation. Such geometries are called cohomogeneity one, and the reduction is the same that makes the Schwarzschild solution easy. The price is an assumption: solutions that break the symmetry are not considered, which the paper expects, without proof, to have higher action.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-fg", "after": "sec-setup-p5", "title": "The Fefferman–Graham expansion and the holographic stress tensor", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Near the boundary of an asymptotically AdS space one can always choose coordinates with $ds^2=(d\rho^2+g_{ij}(x,\rho)dx^idx^j)/\rho^2$. Solving Einstein's equations order by order in $\rho$ gives the Fefferman–Graham expansion: the boundary metric $g_{(0)}$ fixes $g_{(2)}$ and every coefficient up to order $\rho^d$ locally, and in even $d$ a logarithmic term appears there. The coefficient $g_{(d)}$ is not fixed by the near-boundary equations; it is set by regularity deep in the interior, so it carries the information about which solution was chosen. Holographically $g_{(0)}$ is the source, the CFT metric, and $g_{(d)}$ the response: up to a constant and local terms it is the expectation value of the CFT stress tensor. The same expansion is what holographic renormalization uses to cancel the divergences of the action. The paper uses both: it reads the stress tensor off $g_{(d)}$ and integrates the action near the boundary analytically.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-cone", "after": "sec-sing_sol_pert-p3", "title": "Kol's double cone and complex exponents", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A cone over a product of spheres, $ds^2=dr^2+r^2(a^2d\Omega_m^2+b^2d\Omega_n^2)$, is Ricci-flat for $a^2=(m-1)/(m+n-1)$ and $b^2=(n-1)/(m+n-1)$. Kol proposed such a double cone, over $S^2\times S^{D-3}$, as the local geometry where a black string pinches into a black hole, a point at which the topology changes. Linear deformations of a cone are power laws, $r^s$, since the cone has no scale. In $D$ spacetime dimensions the exponents are $s=-\tfrac12\big(D-2\pm\sqrt{(D-2)(D-10)}\big)$, real for $D\ge10$ and complex below. Complex exponents mean deformations that oscillate in $\log r$, so families of solutions approaching the cone spiral rather than arrive monotonically; $D=10$ is a critical dimension. Kalisch, Möckel and Ammon later studied this critical behaviour numerically. Near its tip, where $\sinh z\approx z$, the paper's singular solution is such a cone, with $D=d+1$, so $d<9$ is the oscillating case.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-spiral", "after": "sec-sing_sol_pert-p7", "title": "Log-periodic oscillations and multiplying solutions", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Suppose a boundary quantity behaves along a family of solutions as $\beta-\beta_s\propto r_0^{p}\cos(\omega\log r_0+\phi)$. As $r_0\to0$ these are log-periodic oscillations: each step of $\pi/\omega$ in $\log r_0$ crosses $\beta_s$ once more, with a turning point between crossings, and the amplitude shrinks geometrically. A value of $\beta$ near $\beta_s$ is then met by the family many times, more the closer it is, so the number of solutions grows without bound, multiplying roughly as $\log(1/|\beta-\beta_s|)$. Plotted against a second quantity that oscillates with another phase, the family traces a spiral. Along the family the action is stationary under variations at fixed boundary, so at each turning point of $\beta$ the curve $I(\beta)$ has a cusp, as for small and large AdS black holes at their minimum temperature. Which branch dominates is then decided by the sign of the action's second-order change, which the paper settles numerically.</p>"""},
]
